secure_share": 0.667, "branches": 4, "notes": "Ḫupašiya binds the serpent with a rope (Hitt. išḫiman- = OE sīma, the lexeme sh₂imṓ; Gk. ἱμάς, ἱμάσσω are from the same root *sh₂ey-, the Greek thong with which Zeus lashes the earth around Typhoeus). The verb: Ved. si- (nasal present sinā́ti, Varuṇa's binding, RV 7.84.2) and OE sǣlan 'bind (with rope)' are from the same root; Hittite uses kaleliya-, Greek δέω. Old English is Genesis B (the bound adversary), not in the local corpus. The serpent is interpolated (Hitt. illuyanka- is not cognate). Root-mate sh₂éymn̥ 'rope' (Greek, Celtic reflexes) not used."}
